---
title: Pasta e Ceci
course: main
cuisine: italian
servings: 4
time_active: 20
time_total: 40
leftovers: good
tags: [weeknight, one-pot, vegetarian]
description: Brothy Roman pasta and chickpeas with tomato, rosemary, and kale.
---

Heat @olive oil{3%tbsp} in a #dutch oven{} over medium. Add @shallots{2}(minced; or 1 yellow onion) and a pinch of @salt and cook until soft, ~{4%minutes}.

Stir in @garlic{3%clove}(thinly sliced), @rosemary{2%sprig}(leaves finely chopped; or 1 tsp dried), and @red pepper flakes{1/2%tsp}. Cook until fragrant, ~{1%minute}.

Add @tomato paste{3%tbsp} and cook, stirring, until it darkens to brick red, ~{2%minutes}.

Add @chickpeas{2%can}(15 oz, drained and rinsed) and @chicken broth{5%cups}(or vegetable broth). Bring to a simmer and cook ~{10%minutes}. Mash about a third of the chickpeas against the side of the pot to thicken the broth.

Stir in @pasta{8%oz}(short: ditalini, small shells, or broken spaghetti) and simmer, stirring often so it doesn't stick, until al dente, ~{10-12%minutes}. Add a splash of water if it gets too thick; it should be loose, between a soup and a stew.

Stir in @lacinato kale{1%bunch}(stems removed, chopped) for the last ~{3%minutes}. Season with salt and @black pepper{}.

Serve topped with grated @?parmesan{} and a drizzle of olive oil.

> The pasta keeps drinking the broth: thin leftovers with water or broth when reheating. Keeps 4 days. Swap in white beans for the chickpeas if that's what you have.
